  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Accenture Federal Services recorded $3B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 402 awards, with an average award size of $7.4M and obligations down 7.6% year over year. The company’s obligations have trended upward since FY21 before this year: FY21 $2.5B, FY22 $2.7B, FY23 $2.9B, FY24 $3.4B, FY25 $3.2B, FY26 $3B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date with $525M in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $419M and the Department of Education with $341M. Computer Systems Design Services accounts for the bulk of FY2026 obligations at $2.6B, with Other Computer Related Services and Custom Computer Programming Services at $154M and $92.6M respectively. The top-buying agencies and top NAICS markets show a concentration in IT and professional services, with smaller obligations in markets such as Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $4.5M. These figures reflect fiscal year to date activity and represent contract obligations rather than total contract life values.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Accenture Federal Services' obligations by agency?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $525M for FY2026 to date, making DoD the largest buyer. The Department of Energy obligated $419M and the Department of Education obligated $341M. Ten agencies account for the largest shares, with Department of Commerce at the low end of the top ten with $134M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and services drive Accenture Federal Services' federal revenue?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services drove obligations with $2.6B in FY2026 to date. Other Computer Related Services and Custom Computer Programming Services follow at $154M and $92.6M. Additional services include All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $45.3M and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $30.8M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Accenture Federal Services make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services has $3B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Accenture Federal Services recorded 402 awards so far this fiscal year with an average award size of $7.4M and obligations down 7.6% year over year compared with the prior period."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Accenture Federal Services?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense, Department of Energy, and Department of Education are the top buyers in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $525M, the Department of Energy obligated $419M, the Department of Education obligated $341M, followed by the Department of the Treasury at $267M and the General Services Administration at $255M."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Accenture Federal Services sell to the government?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services sells primarily IT and professional services to the government. Computer Systems Design Services accounts for $2.6B in obligations, Other Computer Related Services accounts for $154M, and Custom Computer Programming Services accounts for $92.6M, with additional obligations in consulting, infrastructure, software publishing, engineering, and smaller manufacturing work such as Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $4.5M."
